From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Mon, 8 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: match the display refresh to the game

VideoPlayer picks a display mode per stream through ChooseBestResolution, so a
25fps film gets a 50Hz screen. RetroPlayer never did - a game ran at whatever
refresh the GUI happened to be on.

A PAL game at 49.92fps against a 59.94Hz screen has every sixth frame shown
twice and its audio stretched by a fifth to fit. The picture survives that; the
sound does not, and 74 of the 349 Saturn discs here are Europe-only releases
with no NTSC equivalent.

The reported rate has to be snapped to a standard one first. Emulators report
their true rate - a PAL Saturn is 49.920128fps - and ChooseBestResolution
matches to a hundredth of a Hz, so the true rate matches no mode at all and the
screen never moves. That is exactly what happened on the first attempt: the
call was made, searched every strategy and logged "No resolution matched".

Honours the same "Adjust display refresh rate" setting VideoPlayer uses, so
turning that off keeps the old behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayer.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayer.cpp
@@ -50,7 +50,12 @@
 #include "utils/StringUtils.h"
 #include "utils/log.h"
 #include "windowing/WinSystem.h"
+#include "settings/Settings.h"
+#include "settings/SettingsComponent.h"
+#include "windowing/GraphicContext.h"
+#include "windowing/Resolution.h"
 
+#include <cmath>
 #include <memory>
 #include <mutex>
 
@@ -204,6 +209,49 @@
 
     // Set video framerate
     m_processInfo->SetVideoFps(static_cast<float>(m_gameClient->GetFrameRate()));
+
+    // Put the screen on a refresh rate that suits the game.
+    //
+    // VideoPlayer does this per stream, so a 25fps film gets a 50Hz screen.
+    // RetroPlayer never did, and a PAL game at 49.92fps on a 59.94Hz screen has
+    // every sixth frame shown twice and its audio stretched by a fifth to fit.
+    // The picture survives that; the sound does not.
+    //
+    // Same call and same setting VideoPlayer uses, so turning off "Adjust
+    // display refresh rate" keeps the old behaviour. A game carries no
+    // dimensions of its own, so the current mode's are passed and only the
+    // refresh moves.
+    // Snap to the nearest broadcast rate before asking.
+    //
+    // Emulators report their true rate - a PAL Saturn is 49.920128fps, not 50 -
+    // and ChooseBestResolution matches to a hundredth of a Hz, so the true rate
+    // matches no mode at all and the screen never moves. The display can only
+    // produce the standard rates anyway, so ask for the one the game is
+    // obviously aiming at and leave anything unusual alone.
+    float gameFps = static_cast<float>(m_gameClient->GetFrameRate());
+    for (const float standard : {23.976f, 24.0f, 25.0f, 29.97f, 30.0f, 50.0f, 59.94f, 60.0f})
+    {
+      if (std::abs(gameFps - standard) < standard * 0.01f)
+      {
+        gameFps = standard;
+        break;
+      }
+    }
+    if (gameFps > 20.0f && gameFps < 130.0f &&
+        CServiceBroker::GetSettingsComponent()->GetSettings()->GetInt(
+            CSettings::SETTING_VIDEOPLAYER_ADJUSTREFRESHRATE) != ADJUST_REFRESHRATE_OFF)
+    {
+      CGraphicContext& gfx = CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetGfxContext();
+      const RESOLUTION_INFO& current = gfx.GetResInfo();
+      const RESOLUTION res = CResolutionUtils::ChooseBestResolution(
+          gameFps, current.iScreenWidth, current.iScreenHeight, false);
+      if (res != gfx.GetVideoResolution())
+      {
+        CLog::Log(LOGINFO, "RetroPlayer[RENDER]: Game runs at {:0.2f} fps, switching display",
+                  gameFps);
+        gfx.SetVideoResolution(res, false);
+      }
+    }
   }
   else
   {
